  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY NIGHT THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 246 AM CDT Tue Sep 1 2026\n\nNot much change is expected late this week as the upper high \nwill retrograde westward across the area and eventually builds \nover the Plains over the weekend. The LREF is showing the \nsurface high remaining anchored over the southeast CONUS which \nwill keep south to southwesterly flow across the area at the \nsame time 850mb temperatures remain around 22C keeping highs and\nconsequently heat indexes nearly the same Thursday and Friday.\n\nThere is no plan to extend the going heat advisory at this point, \nthough it may need to be extended in the future depending on how \nquickly the upper high moves off to the west.  The latest NBM and \nLREF is in relatively good agreement that highs will climb well into \nthe 90s again on Saturday with dewpoints in the 60s resulting in \nanother day with heat indexes climbing above 100 in many locations. \nThere still is some question in how quickly a weak cold front will \nmove into the region from the east over the weekend which will \nprovide some relief from the heat, with the quicker passage bringing \nslightly cooler temperatures on Saturday vs. Sunday.  The front will \nalso bring the potential for moisture pooling which will also \nincrease dewpoints which could raise dewpoints and consequently heat \nindexes most likely on Saturday.  At this point it looks like \nSaturday will still be the hotter day compared to Sunday as there is \nincreasing spread seen in the ensemble output on Saturday vs. Sunday.\n\nEven with a front moving through the area under northwest flow, the \nupper high will act to suppress shower and thunderstorm development \nwith only 25% over the LREF members producing precipitation Saturday \ninto Labor Day.  While some relief is expected behind the front, \nhighs are still forecast to be in the 90s early next week.",
